# cuts/v1.0.27/cut.env -- the pins bin/rollforward_gate.sh binds for --cut v1.0.27
#
# The gate reads exactly TWO keys, and the mapping is DECLARED in the runner:
#     OSTLER_PIN_OA="$(_cut_pin DAEMON_COMMIT)"
#     OSTLER_PIN_CM051="$(_cut_pin CM051)"
# Everything else is prose the gate never touches. Keep it that way: the v1.0.24
# attempt to vendor OS003's fuller records dragged 464 lines of operator working
# notes, four private box IPs and five notarisation IDs into the product repo.
#
# WHY v1.0.27 EXISTS. Two reasons, and the second is the load-bearing one.
#
# (1) THE DAEMON MOVES 0.4.55 -> 0.4.56. v1.0.26 shipped hub-v0.4.55. gui/Makefile
#     now carries DAEMON_VERSION 0.4.56, and #722 re-pinned HUB_APP_SHA256 to the
#     now-published hub-v0.4.56 Ostler.app. Before this cut those two pins had
#     been out of lockstep -- HUB_APP_SHA256 held the hub-v0.4.55 sidecar value
#     while DAEMON_VERSION already said 0.4.56 -- which is the exact shape that
#     ships a daemon and a Hub app from different builds.
#
# (2) A PERSON-NAME SCRUB REACHES THE VENDORED SHIPPING TREE (#725). This is the
#     one that decides whether the DMG is safe to hand to a customer. Earlier
#     passes (#371, #372, #719) reported "153 name-pairs + 72 identifiers to 0",
#     and that was true OF WHAT THEY MEASURED: roughly nine modules and a
#     pair-shaped regex. Against the whole committed tree they missed 11 CM051
#     files, and CM051 is PUBLIC and those files are VENDORED into the shipping
#     tree -- so the residue was on a path to the DMG, not merely to a repo.
#     #725 scrubs them and adds bin/pii_name_guard.py, a PERMIT-LIST rather than
#     a denylist, wired into scripts/run_all_cut_gates.sh.
#
#     MEASURED ON THIS CUT BASE, not inherited from the PR description:
#       python3 bin/pii_name_guard.py --root .  ->  rc=0
#       989 files examined, 7800 candidate tokens, 0 findings in all 4 classes
#       runtime positive control fired (PAIR + PARTICLE + TOKEN + SHAPE arms)
#       and the negative half held (approved cast NOT flagged)
#     THE CAVEAT TRAVELS WITH THE GREEN: 1654 baseline pairs are UNREVIEWED
#     legacy prose. The guard blocks NEW names; it certifies nothing about those.

# The daemon this DMG ships.
#
# v1.0.27 MOVES THE DAEMON 0.4.56 -> 0.4.57, commit acf991bf -> 723c682f.
#
# hub-v0.4.57 is a LIGHTWEIGHT tag in ostler-ai/ostler-assistant: the ref points
# straight at COMMIT 723c682fd7b44b87e3769adfd2db2fdf59f5dadd, so the annotated
# tag-object trap v1.0.26 recorded does not apply here. Re-measured rather than
# inherited, because the shape differs per tag and per repo.
#
# THE PIN NAMES THE SOURCE COMMIT, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING. The
# artefacts ship from TWO repositories: the daemon from ostler-ai/ostler-releases
# and the Hub app from ostler-ai/ostler-assistant. The hub-v0.4.57 tag in the
# RELEASES repo resolves to 14699971, a commit in that artefact-hosting repo,
# which is NOT the source commit and would name a tree the freshness gate cannot
# compare. The gate measures this pin against ostler-ai/ostler-assistant main.
#
# Cross-checked three ways: both published build-info.json files (daemon side and
# Hub-app side) report commit 723c682f, and 723c682f is ostler-assistant main
# HEAD at cut time, so this pin is FRESH rather than merely less stale.
DAEMON_COMMIT=723c682fd7b4

# CM051 pin. The gate reads install.sh AT THIS REF:
#     repos/<CM051_REPO>/contents/install.sh?ref=$CM051_REF
# so the pin must name a tree whose install.sh is the one in the DMG.
#
# 4f3e321 (#714, stage the daemon before the agents that run it) is the most
# recent commit on main that TOUCHES install.sh. Pinning the commit that set the
# file to its final state, rather than whatever main's tip happens to be at tag
# time, is what keeps this key truthful while the merge wave lands: any commit
# that does not touch install.sh cannot invalidate it.
#
# VERIFIED BY BLOB IDENTITY rather than by ancestry alone, because ancestry only
# proves ordering and says nothing about content:
#     git rev-parse 4f3e321:install.sh  -> 7724add8009100c49242e6b06e59defa18ff4fd1
#     git rev-parse 75d228e:install.sh  -> 7724add8009100c49242e6b06e59defa18ff4fd1
# Identical, so the tree this pin names carries byte-for-byte the install.sh that
# is being cut. 4f3e321 is also an ancestor of the cut base (checked, not assumed).
#
# RE-VERIFY THIS IMMEDIATELY BEFORE PUSHING THE TAG. That is the v1.0.26 lesson,
# where a wiki re-pin edited install.sh after the pin was chosen and left the key
# naming a tree whose install.sh was not the one being cut. The check is one
# command: compare the two blob shas above and re-point if they diverge.
CM051=4f3e321
